Solution-Source: https://archive.org/details/sim_judge_1925-01-10_88/page/n34/mode/1up


OCTET#BIS#ROUND
FOE#WHISKEY#SIR
TANGO#POE#ELEGY
#S#E#TEPID#O#H#
STUNT#DON#BOOTH
A#RIOS#D#PEST#O
ME#IDEA#TREE#BT
PRE#DESPAIR#GAL
LR#TIPS#POKE#RI
E#LIES#A#RENO#P
SPATS#FLU#GERMS
#L#L#FLASK#M#O#
HAREM#UBE#SYRUP
ACE#ONTARIO#USA
TEPEE#EMS#WOMEN


A1. Double quartet-not so good since prohibition. ~ OCTET
A5. What you would say if you were in Italy and wanted your glass filled up again. ~ BIS
A8. One drink for all present. ~ ROUND
A12. Enemy. ~ FOE
A13. What the citizens gave the Indians in exchange for Manhattan Island and now wish they could swop back. ~ WHISKEY
A14. Title of respect. ~ SIR
A15. A dance often attempted by the slightly inebriate. ~ TANGO
A17. Famous American writer who would not have joined the anti-saloon league. ~ POE
A18. Appropriate song after a drink of wood alcohol. ~ ELEGY
A20. The way both a hot toddy and a frosted mint julep get if they aren't drunk when they should be. ~ TEPID
A21. What is balancing a beer bottle on your nose and playing a piano at the same time? ~ STUNT
A24. Masculine Spanish title of rank. ~ DON
A25. Maker of an excellent gin; also a private place to drink it. ~ BOOTH
A28. The proper places for water (Spanish). ~ RIOS
A30. One who is always talking about what a good drinker he is. ~ PEST
A31. One of the first States to go dry (abr.) ~ ME
A33. A mental image—you get a lot of these after a few drinks. ~ IDEA
A35. Origin of wood alcohol. ~ TREE
A36. Bottle (abr.) ~ BT
A37. Prefix that means quality in bibulous circles. ~ PRE
A38. State of mind of bartenders when prohibition went into effect. ~ DESPAIR
A39. What four quarts make (abr.). ~ GAL
A40. Liquid rejuvenator (abr.). ~ LR
A41. Something a canoe does that a Scotchman doesn't. ~ TIPS
A42. Something a prohibition agent often gets in the eye. ~ POKE
A44. A New England State that refused to ratify the Eighteenth Amendment (abr.). ~ RI
A45. What a bootlegger does when he claims his goods are genuine pre-war. ~ LIES
A47. City of divorces. ~ RENO
A49. Articles of raiment worn by sporty bootleggers. ~ SPATS
A51. A disease said to be curable by liquor (abr.). ~ FLU
A53. Causes of the above disease that don't like alcohol. ~ GERMS
A55. A hip-pocket necessity. ~ FLASK
A56. A popular organization of ladies in Turkey. ~ HAREM
A59. United Bootleggers' Employees (abr.). ~ UBE
A60. A kind of drink much in favor with drugstore cowboys ~ SYRUP
A63. A high-flyer that's good. ~ ACE
A64. Dry province of a neighboring country. ~ ONTARIO
A65. The land of the spree and the home of the crave (abr). ~ USA
A66. Indian place for consuming fire water. ~ TEPEE
A67. Measurements used by printers (perhaps of labels) ~ EMS
A68. Wives of drinkers. ~ WOMEN

D1. Poetical expression for the time to take a drink. ~ OFT
D2. Rum-runners destination. ~ COAST
D3. Two less than a case. ~ TEN
D4. One drink and then one more. ~ TWO
D5. A two-legged drinker, scientifically speaking. ~ BIPED
D6. Eight-legged crustacean (If you start seeing them it's time to swear off). ~ ISOPOD
D7. According to Webster a quantity of thread coiled together. ~ SKEIN
D8. American brand and hard to get. ~ RYE
D9. Something to do with your stock on hand. ~ USE
D10. Every dog has its day, but this is reserved for the cats and rum-runners. ~ NIGHT
D11. What the United States is not. ~ DRY
D16. The plural of what Aladdin said came out of his lamp. (They used to get 'em in those days, too.) ~ GENII
D19. A Scotchman may get very tight but he never gets this way. ~ LOOSE
D21. Things you can't take home if you work in a bank. bank. ~ SAMPLES
D22. United Rummies (abr.). ~ UR
D23. Kind of drink served hot (pl.). ~ TODDIES
D25. Container for beverage brewed from hops (two words). ~ BEERKEG
D26. Old timers (abr.). ~ OT
D27. Something you get when you put the wrong end of a cigar in your mouth (two words—pl.) ~ HOTLIPS
D29. Funk & Wagnall's says to drain liquid by drawing (pl.). (There are other ways to empty a keg.) ~ SEEPS
D30. Before prohibition or before anything. ~ PRIOR
D32. You do this, if you drink too much. ~ ERR
D34. A long-eared animal. ~ ASS
D35. Something you do to a keg to get it open. ~ TAP
D36. The thing we used to hang our umbrellas on in the good old daze. ~ BAR
D41. What "Ten Nights in a Bar Room" is when it's written on the cover of a book. ~ TITLE
D43. Anyone who won't drink with you. ~ ENEMY
D45. State famous for absinthe drips and zazarac (abr.). ~ LA
D46. Another Southern State; spelled the way they sing it in the cabarets. ~ ALABAM
D48. Correlative of either. ~ OR
D50. A special spot or locality. ~ PLACE
D51. Musical instrument that the player is very liable to be shot from under. ~ FLUTE
D52. Consumers. ~ USERS
D54. The animal that took a drink of bootleg and then went out looking for the cat. ~ MOUSE
D56. The thing a souse always manages to sit on before the evening is over. ~ HAT
D57. What a chorus girl gets if she drinks too much (abr.). ~ REP
D58. First name of a famous prohibition agent. ~ MOE
D60. Lady pig (not a blind one). ~ SOW
D61. Popular beverage of the West Indies and New York. ~ RUM
D62. To speak ill of your bootlegger or anyone. ~ PAN


Printed with puzzle:
Judge will run a Crossword Puzzle every week and will pay $25 for each one used on this page—but they must be funny. Others accepted will be paid for at regular rates. Puzzles will not be returned unless postage is enclosed.

